{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-format.md\", \"total_lines\": 426, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 108, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 0, \"requested_end\": 108, \"has_more\": true, \"next_start\": 109, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": 358, \"text\": \"1: # NL0 data contract, version 2\\n2: \\n3: This document specifies representation. Mathematical acceptance comes from\\n4: [Audit A's shared requirements](audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements).\\n5: The clean carrier binds the shared section/version/hash in execution and\\n6: author-readiness records; these are controller records, not extra proof axioms\\n7: or new fields to invent inside spec/graph.\\n8: \\n9: New graphs/specifications use format v2 and Yui audit records use v4. The existing v1\\n10: Koch/BO cases are historical snapshots and have not been reaccepted. Reading\\n11: them requires `--legacy`; current default checks reject them. Historical\\n12: receipt validity is labeled `LEGACY-*` and `current_standard_receipt_valid`\\n13: remains false. Do not mechanically bump a version number to migrate a proof.\\n14: Apply [self-containment](self-containment.md) to the actual mathematical text.\\n15: \\n16: ## Authority and semantics\\n17: \\n18: The isolated authoring carrier offers `check_draft` and `submit_draft`, taking\\n19: an `artifact` JSON object directly. Prefer these to the compatible older\\n20: `artifact_json` string wrappers: an additional string-encoding layer is not\\n21: part of the mathematical format. Both routes execute identical checks.\\n22: \\n23: `spec.json` freezes the original full theorem, the conclusion under its declared\\n24: parameters/hypotheses, the exact H list, global definition pairs, logic policy,\\n25: source-input hashes, and Lean/mathlib pins. A human must confirm that this\\n26: specification matches the original problem; string equality cannot establish\\n27: semantic fidelity. `nl0.json` references this spec by its SHA256 and duplicates\\n28: none of its definitions or H statements. One separate spec is required per\\n29: target. Multiple-target decomposition is outside version 2.\\n30: \\n31: Read a derived proposition as a theorem under the original parameters and the\\n32: H nodes actually used on its derivation path. All additional free variables are\\n33: locally quantified in its statement; there are no ambient undeclared claims.\\n34: An unused original H is allowed and reported; do not silently strengthen or\\n35: weaken the frozen theorem. E/M results retain their general signatures; apply\\n36: them through explicit bindings and hypothesis checks in the inference.\\n37: \\n38: Graph vertices are propositions and inferences. Edges are generated only as\\n39: `input proposition -> inference -> output proposition`. An inference requires\\n40: all inputs. List order is presentation only: a dependency may occur later in\\n41: the JSON array, provided the graph admits a topological order. Actual circular\\n42: dependencies, unknown IDs, and disconnected retained branches fail.\\n43: \\n44: The isolated authoring carrier also reports a hash-bound machine checkpoint of\\n45: its latest own draft and actual structural result. After a context reset, resume\\n46: that candidate with `read_draft`/`base_candidate`; do not claim no draft exists or\\n47: restart the frozen input accidentally. The checkpoint grants no Audit A verdict.\\n48: \\n49: ## Specification fields\\n50: \\n51: Use [the authoring template](../templates/authoring.md) and the complete\\n52: [model-visible submission example](../templates/submission-example.md). The\\n53: example explains metadata placeholders and local-witness accounting; it is not\\n54: a benchmark or proof certification. Required top-level spec fields are:\\n55: \\n56: - `format_version: 2`, `case_id`, `full_target`, `conclusion` (nonempty strings);\\n57: - `parameters`: records `{symbol, type}`; parameter symbols reserve global names.\\n58:   Give ambient data types/structures only. Original conditions, ranges and\\n59:   equations belong in H, not in parameter refinements that would be visible\\n60:   to every R;\\n61: - `hypotheses`: records `{id: Hddd, statement, origin}` extracted before authoring;\\n62: - `definitions`: records `{id: Dddd, symbol, definition, type, depends_on,\\n63:   requires, binders, objects}`. Each symbol has exactly one global meaning. `depends_on` lists\\n64:   earlier/resolvable D IDs and is acyclic. `requires` lists proved H/N IDs that\\n65:   must already be available whenever the definition is used;\\n66: - `logic`: `{classical: boolean, rules: [names], calculations: [fixed IDs],\\n67:   primitives: [{symbol, type, meaning}]}`. In the clean author tool, omit\\n68:   `calculations` and `primitives` to bind the controller's fixed language profile.\\n69:   Optional calculation IDs are `equality`, `definitions`, `finite_algebra`,\\n70:   `finite_order`, `finite_sums`. Optional primitive selections may be\\n71:   `{symbol: ...}`; the controller validates selections and binds the complete\\n72:   core syntax with exact type/meaning (numerals, number systems and empty-set/top\\n73:   notation). This is not a subject vocabulary or a list of allowed theorems. A new\\n74:   Audit A requires the bound metadata. Author-defined permission descriptions\\n75:   and conflicting primitive meanings are rejected, not silently accepted;\\n76: - `pins`: `{lean_version, mathlib_commit, mathlib_root}`; the root is a local\\n77:   locator, the version/commit are scientific identifiers;\\n78: - `inputs`: evidence records `{path, sha256}` for the original problem/proof.\\n79: \\n80: The exact machine identifiers permitted in `logic.rules` and inference `rules`\\n81: are `direct`, `modus_ponens`, `implication_introduction`, `negation_introduction`,\\n82: `contradiction_classical`, `case_elimination`, `forall_introduction`,\\n83: `exists_elimination`, and `natural_induction`. Describe algebra/analysis in the\\n84: actual proof text; it is not a new trusted logical rule. These identifiers\\n85: describe proof packaging, not a substitute for establishing the implication.\\n86: \\n87: The `elementary-language-4` profile in `nl0/scripts/foundations.py` permits\\n88: displayed finite algebra/order calculations and ordinary logic. Its core syntax\\n89: contains no domain-specific vocabulary. Objects and notation from any subject\\n90: are parameters, D definitions or scoped locals, with explicit meaning, type and\\n91: dependencies; they cannot be hidden in primitives. Recognizable notation grants\\n92: no identity, existence or regularity. No global dictionary extension is needed\\n93: to represent a new subject.\\n94: For every substantive fact still register an E/M input or give its local proof.\\n95: \\n96: Older saved specifications retain their original bytes and symbol metadata for\\n97: read-only inspection under their recorded runtime. Starting a new Audit A under\\n98: this profile requires current canonical metadata: remove obsolete domain\\n99: primitive entries, declare any objects they actually supplied, and validate\\n100: the resulting source again. A new profile does not upgrade an old receipt.\\n101: \\n102: Definitions may be globally visible but guarded. Example: “K is the unique\\n103: fixed curve from N008” requires N008 before K is used. N008 itself must state\\n104: existence using the previously defined space/operator, without assuming K.\\n105: Proof prerequisites must be direct inference inputs in v2;\\n106: the output being proved cannot discharge its own definition prerequisite.\\n107: Definition prerequisites of input/output statements are checked too.\\n108: \"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-format.md\", \"total_lines\": 426, \"start\": 251, \"end\": 426, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 53, \"requested_end\": 500, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"251: ## Inference fields\\n252: \\n253: Required fields: `id: Rddd`, `inputs` (unique proposition IDs), `output` (one\\n254: N/P ID), `definitions` (direct D references), `rules` (permitted logic-rule\\n255: names), `locals`, `argument`, and `applications`.\\n256: \\n257: - `locals`: `{symbol, type, scope}` records. Scope identifies a binding inside\\n258:   this inference; names cannot shadow globals. Two bindings in different\\n259:   scopes/nodes may use the same spelling. Bound variables of source theorems\\n260:   can be alpha-renamed and instantiated. Audit actual binding/freshness.\\n261:   In v2 also require `start`, `end` (inclusive S IDs), `kind` (`arbitrary`,\\n262:   `definition`, or `witness`), `declaration` (full introduction), `from_inputs`,\\n263:   `from_local` (prior F IDs) and `objects` (already available objects used to\\n264:   introduce this one). A witness must have an input/local existence basis.\\n265:   The checker verifies declared availability/order, not the existence claim's\\n266:   semantics. Source-bound variables need a legitimate local instantiation.\\n267:   The `scope` label and inclusive start/end must agree with the prose. Keep an\\n268:   object live through its last actual use. Establish an existence F in an\\n269:   earlier S, then choose its witness in a subsequent S; using a newly produced\\n270:   F as the witness basis in the same paragraph is a forward reference.\\n271: - `argument`: ordered records `{id: Sddd, text, uses, uses_local, objects,\\n272:   produces, introduces, discharges}`. S IDs are inference-local and unique. `uses`\\n273:   lists direct graph inputs used at this point. Local facts established earlier\\n274:   in the prose do not become graph inputs. The union of `uses` must equal\\n275:   `inputs`. A citation's presence is not semantic evidence that it was used.\\n276:   `uses_local` lists only earlier F IDs; `objects` lists the actually used\\n277:   global/active local objects. `produces` holds complete local facts\\n278:   `{id: Fddd, statement, binders, objects, assumptions}`; assumptions lists remaining\\n279:   local L IDs on which the fact depends. A local fact cannot be used before\\n280:   its production or outside its assumption/object scope. A paragraph cannot\\n281:   cite its newly produced F as a previous premise. Formulas to be reused by a\\n282:   different R must appear in the exported proposition, not only in `produces`.\\n283: - `introduces`/`discharges`: lists of inference-local assumption IDs (e.g.\\n284:   `L1`). Temporary assumptions are never proposition vertices. Every introduced\\n285:   ID is unique in this inference and must be discharged once, no earlier than\\n286:   introduction. These lists account for prose; Audit A checks the actual\\n287:   formula, scope, and chosen rule. A correctly closed implication may retain\\n288:   the assumption in its antecedent. Deleting the ID cannot justify an output.\\n289: - `applications`: records covering every directly used E/M input, with\\n290:   `{input, at: Sddd, bindings, hypothesis_checks}`.\\n291:   `bindings` is a nonempty string spelling out the actual variable/parameter\\n292:   substitution; it is not a JSON mapping. `hypothesis_checks` is an ordered\\n293:   list of nonempty strings, one per declared source obligation. For example,\\n294:   `\\\"bindings\\\": \\\"Set the source parameter a to the current x.\\\"` records a\\n295:   substitution; it does not prove its conditions.\\n296:   The S paragraph must list\\n297:   this input in `uses`; only local facts available before that paragraph may\\n298:   supply application conditions. E applications additionally require\\n299:   an inference-local unique `id: Addd`; different actual uses of the same E\\n300:   need separate IDs and records. M may also use explicit A IDs for repeated\\n301:   instances; a legacy M without `id` uses its input ID as the unique key.\\n302:   `hypothesis_checks` has one nonempty\\n303:   explanation for each corresponding source obligation. A hypothesis needs an\\n304:   actual argument from inputs/H/local proved facts; filling a string is only\\n305:   an accounting check. Prove substantive normalization bridges as N nodes.\\n306: \\n307: Input-free R nodes are permitted only for explic…3211 tokens truncated…. Keep all required coefficients,\\n63: normalizations, limiting arguments and exceptional cases.\\n64: Retain every calculation needed for P and the supplied core route. An\\n65: independent optional comment/corollary outside that dependency may be omitted;\\n66: note a material omission in the existing context note. This never licenses\\n67: dropping a needed derivation or weakening an output. Any retained claim must\\n68: itself have its complete grounds.\\n69: \\n70: ## 3. Construct the proof and its factual grounds together\\n71: \\n72: An isomorphism or structural identification supplies its stated maps and\\n73: relations, not every subsequent dimension/rank claim by name alone. For a\\n74: needed count, display the basis, spanning/independence and finite count, or\\n75: invoke an appropriate verified source. Likewise regularity closure and\\n76: differentiation rules need their actual grounds; a familiar operation name\\n77: does not extend the fixed calculation profile. Prefer an adequate verified\\n78: background input to unnecessarily rebuilding its whole theory.\\n79: \\n80: For each S, identify its substantive direct inputs and local grounds while\\n81: writing. Ordinary correct elementary reasoning may be compressed; routine mathematical facts may receive concise mathematically reviewed\\n82: background notes, without exact Mathlib lookup. Check the\\n83: unit at that point; do not postpone its source inventory until after the whole\\n84: proof. Read `dependency_closure` and `argument` as construction obligations, not final assurances to fill in later.\\n85: \\n86: If a fact needs an M/E, inspect the real pinned/primary source, register its\\n87: full contract, and write its actual application. Follow `sources` and\\n88: `applicability`: substitute the actual function/domain/scalars and ground each\\n89: premise in an available input or displayed derivation. A source-hypothesis\\n90: field saying a condition holds is not itself a derivation. Prefer an already\\n91: proved adequate general theorem to rebuilding it. A specialized bridge still\\n92: needs its local proof. For E follow [external-source review](external-theorems.md).\\n93: For inaccessible text follow Audit A's [retrieval/deferred checks](audit-a.md#paywalled-or-inaccessible-primary-text).\\n94: \\n95: M is optional. Do not search native declarations for every routine fact before\\n96: NL0 review; use precise mathematical reasoning and the background-note policy.\\n97: If M is explicitly selected, use the actual pinned declaration as the full source contract, including\\n98: its binders, implicit arguments and typeclass premises. Do not insert an\\n99: extra condition into that contract or omit a native premise. Keep a specialized\\n100: application distinct from the native statement and justify its substitutions.\\n101: The probe supplies canonical source metadata; it does not discharge the\\n102: application's premises or certify the local proof.\\n103: \\n104: Write the exact conclusion obtained at an application before using it at a\\n105: different domain, measure, carrier or codomain. Any restriction, extension or\\n106: identification requires its own available ground. If instead deriving a fact\\n107: by a named property's definition, supply that definition and display the\\n108: instantiated clause and ensuing reasoning. Do not register a new source just\\n109: because a fully displayed definition-based derivation also has a standard\\n110: name. Prove each added consequence under all its cases; omit an unnecessary\\n111: extra claim rather than leave its proof incomplete.\\n112: \\n113: Introduce an auxiliary object at its actual first use, with its kind,\\n114: existence basis and scope. Keep object dependencies in `objects` and earlier\\n115: proof IDs in `from_local`. Preserve the object's type and all uses when\\n116: renaming it. Apply `definitions` and the grouped `statement_closure` check to the actual prose.\\n117: Use [logical packaging](logical-rules.md) for quantified and conditional work.\\n118: \\n119: ## 4. Inspect the actual stored candidate\\n120: \\n121: Use check_draft for structural feedback and read_draft for actual changed\\n122: records. Continue from the same-stage candidate after a rejected edit. Stable\\n123: unique @ID/@symbol selectors are available in own-stage reads/edits, not in\\n124: Audit A evidence pointers. Format work requires a concrete edit or source\\n125: query rather than repeated progress reports.\\n126: For multiline text use read_draft's numbered line view and its complete-string\\n127: hash with replace_lines; avoid retyping an escaped old paragraph merely to\\n128: make a small edit. Re-read after a stale hash and use existing @IDs after\\n129: array changes. No editor infers missing mathematics.\\n130: \\n131: Read each own conditional packet after structural checking. It supplies only\\n132: that R's actual premises, definitions, fixed logic, output and own argument.\\n133: Use the shared criteria to inspect these contents, including each output's\\n134: consumer-facing statement, rather than remembering what the full manuscript\\n135: or another producer established. This is the same author's inspection, not an\\n136: extra independent reviewer and not a second Audit A.\\n137: Apply Audit A's shared scope rule: an actual direct N is assumed in this\\n138: conditional implication; do not re-prove its producer or read an ancestor\\n139: body here. The global graph must still contain that producer, and a needed\\n140: fact absent from the exported statement must be established explicitly.\\n141: In the existing original_context_note explicitly check the actual D records'\\n142: types and guards against their available producers, as well as H/target and\\n143: source fidelity. In the inference notes compare each source's exact delivered\\n144: conclusion with the next line that consumes it. These are inspections of the\\n145: stored proof, not another copy of its argument.\\n146: \\n147: Record concise located context/output/inference notes using the existing\\n148: fields. Their criterion grouping comes directly from Audit A. Report readiness\\n149: as no_known_issue, unresolved or not_checked and preserve located open issues.\\n150: A complete set of notes with an unresolved issue cannot release the proof.\\n151: Resolve it in the actual stored argument, or explicitly justify withdrawing\\n152: an erroneous self-finding; reference the resolution location. Do not erase an\\n153: old issue by omitting it. Python validates these states and bindings, not the\\n154: truth of the author's mathematical judgment. A claim of no_known_issue can\\n155: still fail the subsequent independent Audit A.\\n156: \\n157: ### Routine completion and substantive proof gaps\\n158: \\n159: A needed statement in the supplied proof is initially a proof obligation:\\n160: derive it locally, export a proved N, or supply an exactly verified source.\\n161: The fact that the current draft has not done so yet is not a mathematical\\n162: obstruction. Missing inventory, source registration or exposition is work to\\n163: complete, not by itself a terminal gap report. If reporting an actual obstacle,\\n164: identify the concrete implication and the mathematical difficulty encountered\\n165: during normal understanding, checking and completion, rather than only noting\\n166: that no proof/source has yet been entered. This imposes no fixed time/attempt\\n167: count and requires neither a counterexample nor a proof of irreparability.\\n168: \\n169: When writing NL0, expand the argument along the proof strategy already present\\n170: in the original work. Actively supply routine omitted derivations, correct\\n171: verifiable typographical errors and non-substantive local mistakes, and write\\n172: the complete argument into NL0 without interrupting the user for each item.\\n173: Completion must preserve the original target, the original theorem hypotheses\\n174: and the core proof strategy.\\n175: \\n176: If a step remains blocked after normal understanding, checking and completion\\n177: attempts, carefully assess the obstacle: is it work needed for ordinary\\n178: rigorous expansion, or a substantive gap in the original proof? The model\\n179: should make this judgment from the mathematical content and actual progress;\\n180: no fixed elapsed-time limit or attempt count is a mathematical criterion.\\n181: \\n182: If the obstacle is judged to be a substantive proof gap, stop NL0 construction\\n183: for the current proof, preserve the completed work, and report the gap's\\n184: location, the unsupported implication and the grounds for that judgment.\\n185: Do not automatically start new research on the original problem, seek a new\\n186: core proof strategy, or modify the original theorem to make the proof pass.\\n187: \\n188: Recognizing a gap does not require finding a counterexample or proving that\\n189: the gap is irreparable. If the only established fact is that completion has\\n190: not been achieved, and its substantive nature cannot be judged reliably,\\n191: report an \\\"unresolved suspected proof gap\\\" rather than declaring the original\\n192: theorem false. Prolonged runtime, network or tool faults, and model capability\\n193: limitations alone are not evidence of a mathematical error.\\n194: \\n195: In the isolated NL0 carrier, unfinished source registration, JSON bookkeeping or\\n196: ordinary drafting calls for the next actual query, draft or local edit, not an\\n197: obstacle report. Keep the own draft and continue within its remaining budget. A\\n198: terminal mathematical-gap report must identify a genuinely unsupported\\n199: mathematical implication and its grounds, rather than merely restating that\\n200: the requested artifact is not finished. A stage outcome is not whole-task\\n201: completion; ordinary failed candidates return to bounded correction/review.\\n202: \\n203: Incorporate completed corrections into the argument and its actual dependency\\n204: records. Audit A reviews that revised version; existing hash binding and\\n205: invalidation rules still apply.\\n206: \\n207: \\n208: ## 5. Freeze and hand over\\n209: \\n210: When there is no known unfinished obligation, submit the exact candidate bound\\n211: to the author notes and shared acceptance version. If the budget ends first,\\n212: preserve the draft as unfinished; do not call a known-incomplete artifact a\\n213: complete proof. A diagnostic audit of an unfinished draft, if requested,\\n214: remains separate from a qualifying first-acceptance audit.\\n215: \\n216: Freeze source/graph hashes and generate views and an UNVERIFIED scaffold.\\n217: Audit A receives the original problem for fidelity and fresh per-R packets for\\n218: local replay; it receives no author notes or prior review feedback. Its actual\\n219: judgments determine acceptance under the same shared criteria. Structural\\n220: validation, author readiness and mathematical acceptance are distinct results.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/templates/submission-example.md\", \"total_lines\": 222, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 222, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 42, \"requested_end\": 250, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # Complete NL0 v2 submission example\\n2: \\n3: This is a neutral format example, not a reference solution to any exercise or\\n4: an accepted Audit A. It is available through `read_yui`; test fixtures and old\\n5: proofs remain inaccessible. Copy the data shape, not its mathematical contents.\\n6: The clean NL0 tool binds `inputs`, `pins` and `spec_sha256` before checking.\\n7: It also binds omitted fixed calculation/primitive metadata. All mathematical\\n8: statements and proof fields are authored and reviewed. Empty arrays are intentional.\\n9: The clean tool also permits omitting redundant H/P statements and E/M\\n10: `source.statement`: it copies their primary spec/proposition statement.\\n11: Conflicting supplied duplicates are rejected. The canonical files always\\n12: contain the complete texts, and source fidelity still needs actual review.\\n13: \\n14: ```json\\n15: {\\n16:   \\\"spec\\\": {\\n17:     \\\"format_version\\\": 2,\\n18:     \\\"case_id\\\": \\\"closed-addition-format-example\\\",\\n19:     \\\"full_target\\\": \\\"For natural numbers a and b, if a=b, then a+0=b.\\\",\\n20:     \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"a+0=b.\\\",\\n21:     \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n22:       {\\n23:         \\\"symbol\\\": \\\"a\\\",\\n24:         \\\"type\\\": \\\"natural number\\\"\\n25:       },\\n26:       {\\n27:         \\\"symbol\\\": \\\"b\\\",\\n28:         \\\"type\\\": \\\"natural number\\\"\\n29:       }\\n30:     ],\\n31:     \\\"hypotheses\\\": [\\n32:       {\\n33:         \\\"id\\\": \\\"H001\\\",\\n34:         \\\"statement\\\": \\\"a=b.\\\",\\n35:         \\\"origin\\\": \\\"problem.txt:1\\\"\\n36:       }\\n37:     ],\\n38:     \\\"definitions\\\": [],\\n39:     \\\"log